      intro:
        "A dessert name should taste sweet before the first bite: this category sells emotional reward — nobody walks into a dessert shop out of hunger, they walk in because today deserves a treat, and the name has to deliver the first spoonful the moment it's spoken. The first material bin is sweet imagery: sugar, honey, cream, snow, clouds, puffs — words that carry texture and temperature on their own; but beware that the whole industry draws from the same jar, and names piled from stock sweet-words taste generic. The second lever is texture synesthesia: write the mouthfeel into the name — velvety, crackly, molten, swirled — so the name itself can be eaten; Häagen-Dazs ran the opposite play, inventing a fake Nordic word to sell imported luxury, proving dessert synesthesia can be atmosphere rather than flavor. The third is emotional reward: dessert sits at the center of the small-happiness economy — names can state the feeling outright, and in the social-media era a dessert name is half the caption already. Mind the scene split: street stalls need shoutable names, mall shops need photogenic ones, and packaged ice-cream brands need names that tell a story on a box — one category, three naming logics. On channels: dessert lives in afternoon-tea proposals, discovery posts and delivery lists — the name must surface first at the moment of \"I want something sweet,\" or lose to the chain downstairs.",
