      intro:
        "A car rental name should pull away the moment you say it: this category turns \"leave whenever\" into a service — customers rent the freedom and command of a journey, not a machine, and the name must deliver \"we're set\" certainty at the trip-planning moment. The first material bin is freedom on the road: road, route, drive, wind — the emotional core of self-driving is the wheel in your own hands, and the name can carry road-movie imagery; but freedom must land on reliability, because handing your trip to an unknown brand is a trust leap. The second lever is the reliability promise: rental's decision pain is fear of traps — vehicle condition, deposits, one-way returns, accident disputes — so the name's air of legitimacy participates directly in the decision; Hertz presses a century of family-firm trust into one surname syllable, while China Auto Rental writes nationwide reliability into a name with national gravitas. The third is speed and efficiency: pickup-and-return speed is the new generation's core pitch — Sixt's clipped punch and eHi's single bright syllable transmit service tempo through pure sound; two syllables, open vowels and a light step are the category's phonetic playbook. Mind the model split: branch-based rental competes on locations and fleet, P2P sharing (Turo-style) competes on community and character cars, subscriptions compete on membership — three models, three registers, and a platform name needs headroom for category expansion (rental to chauffeured to road-trip packages). Business travelers want receipts and speed, tourists want itineraries and models, locals want a weekend seven-seater — the name must stand in OTA price-comparison lists and on arrival-hall lightboxes alike. On channels: rental lives in trip-planning searches, airport signage and \"the car I got at X was solid\" word of mouth — the name must say and search cleanly, and steer clear of sound-alike collisions with the giants.",
